so far we've defined work in one  dimension but let's consider the path of  a particle in more than one dimension on  this screen it looks like a  two-dimensional path and here's our  particle and what we'd like to do is  define the concept of work for this type  of path now let's say that this instant  our particle is experiencing a force F  and the particle is being displaced by a  distance D s which is always tangent to  the path and what we'd like to do now is  talk about the work done for this  particle now what we'd like to first say  is what we're going to do is imagine  that we're gonna break the path down  into a bunch of small intervals and  let's call this the J the point and the  J plus 1 point then our particle will  start at J and will displace it Delta s  J to the J plus 1 position on the path  and here let's denote the force by FJ  and now what we'd like to do is define  the work for this particular section now  our concept of work is the force in the  direction of the displacement so what  I'd like to do is I'd like to define the  quantity F J and I'll put a little  parentheses upstairs a parallel lines  upstairs this is the component of the  force F J in the direction of Delta s J  now the way we denote that is we can  then say that if I take the dot product  of F J dot Delta s J this is the  component of the force in the direction  of the motion because remember our dot  product if we call this angle theta J is  taking how much of one vector is in the  direction of the other times the  magnitude of that displacement and again  when we write that component in  notation if we call this angle theta J  we know that that's the magnitude of F J  times sine of cosine of theta J now  because this is the amount of force in  the direction of the motion that's how  we've defined work done and we'll  symbolize the work done in taking the  particle from J - J plus 1 by the symbol  W J and now this is a scalar quantity  and when we want to find the total work  what we have to add up is from some  initial point to some final point how  much work is being done in taking our  particle from the initial position to  the final position and it's just the sum  for all of these J's and let's say we  have n of them of WJ and right now we're  going to call this W is just that scalar  sum and that's equal to the sum j goes  from 1 to N of F J dot Delta s J so now  we'll use the vector dot product because  we already know that this is how much of  the force is in the direction of the  motion however this answer depends on  how fine we broke up this path and what  we've seen many times now is that the  actual work that we want let's write  this as n because it depends on the  number of paths is the limit as n goes  to infinity of this sum of scalar  amounts of work now when we take that  limit as n goes to infinity of this sum  J equals 1 to N of F J dot Delta s J  this quantity is formally what we mean  by a line integral and we denote that  with a new notation we'll denote a line  from our initial to final and it's a dot  product of the force dotted into the  small displace  now whenever we see the dot product in  an integral that distinguishes it from a  normal integral because and this is what  we call a line integral now again why  did we switch from our Delta s s to DS  is because when we take the limit as n  goes to infinity  our grid becomes finer and finer and  finer until we're shrinking down to a  point where we can just talk about the  differential displacement DS and the  force acting on a point and that's what  our line integral is now what we'll  learn neck next is how to calculate this  line integral for a number of important  physical examples that occur in our  interest in mechanics
 

